          SECTION 0 — DEFINITIONS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

"OC1" means OpenComputers for Forge 1.12.2 and
earlier. Lua 5.2/5.3. OpenOS. Robots. Drones.
Graphics cards. A real filesystem. Component APIs.
The ability to automate your entire base with
a hundred lines of Lua at 2am.
This was good. This was correct. This was enough.

"OC2" means OpenComputers II by Sangar (fnuecke).
A mod in which your Minecraft computer boots Linux
over an emulated 64-bit RISC-V architecture,
communicates with Minecraft via JSON-RPC over a
virtual serial device, and requires you to write
kernel-adjacent code to turn on a lamp.

    The author acknowledges this is technically
    impressive.
    The author does not care.
    You are playing Minecraft.

"OC2R" means OpenComputers II: Reimagined, a fork
of OC2 that continues OC2's tradition of running
a real operating system inside a game about
punching trees, now on newer Minecraft versions.
The "Reimagined" refers to imagining that anyone
asked for this.

"Sedna" means the RISC-V emulation library
powering OC2 and OC2R, written entirely in Java,
capable of booting Linux, and living inside a
Minecraft mod.

    Sedna is, objectively, an extraordinary
    engineering achievement.
    It has absolutely no business being here.

"The Good Times" means the era in which OC1 was
maintained, robots roamed the land, and you could
automate a sorting system without installing a
bootloader.

"Software" means this mod, which ports OC1 to
modern Forge, because someone had to do it.

